---
import ArticleShell from '../../components/ArticleShell.astro';
import { claim, articleFacts } from '../../lib/articles.js';
import { withArticle } from '../../lib/grammar.js';
import { STAGES } from '../../lib/hue.js';

/**
 * Artikel: duur van het werkzame leven. Het nieuws in de data: een reeks zonder breuk sinds
 * 2000, en het werkzame leven is in vrijwel elk land langer geworden.
 */
const { h, u, g, y, source, fmt } = articleFacts('working-life');
const W = (name: string) => withArticle(name);
const top = u.rose[0];
const fell = u.fell;
const womenLonger = g.womenAbove.map((c: any) => W(c.name));
const euChange = y && y.euPrev !== null ? h.eu.total - y.euPrev : null;
/** De tussenkop noemt de Baltische staten alleen als de data precies die drie aanwijst. */
const baltic = g.womenAbove.map((c: any) => c.code).sort().join() === 'EE,LT,LV';

// Zinnen die niet uit één getal volgen; zie claim() in src/lib/articles.js.
claim(u.rose.length >= u.countries * 0.9, 'Almost every country went the same way');

const title = `Working lives are longer in ${u.rose.length} of ${u.countries} European countries than in ${u.from}`;
const description =
  `Someone starting out in the EU can now expect ${fmt(h.eu.total)} years of working life (${h.year}). ` +
  `Since ${u.from} it has grown in ${u.rose.length} of ${u.countries} countries, most in ${W(top.name)}.`;
---

<ArticleShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/notes/working-life-longer-europe/"
  hue={STAGES.retire}
  published="2026-11-21"
  metricPath="/working-life/"
  metricLabel="Working life"
  cta="See the expected working life in every country"
  source={source}
>
  <p slot="lead">
    A 15-year-old in the European Union can now expect <b>{fmt(h.eu.total)} years</b> in the labour
    market, according to Eurostat's figures for {h.year}.
    {euChange !== null && <> That is {fmt(Math.abs(euChange))} years {euChange >= 0 ? 'more' : 'less'} than a year earlier.</>}
    {' '}And in {u.rose.length} of the {u.countries} countries with an unbroken series, working life is longer
    than it was in {u.from}.
  </p>

  <h2>Almost every country went the same way</h2>
  <p>
    Few European figures move this uniformly. Eurostat has measured the expected duration of working
    life without a single series break since {u.from}. Over that period it grew in {u.rose.length} countries
    {fell.length === 0 ? <> and fell in none.</> : fell.length === 1 ? <> and fell in one: {W(fell[0].name)}, by {fmt(Math.abs(fell[0].change))} years.</> : <> and fell in {fell.length}.</>}
  </p>
  <p>
    The biggest change was in {W(top.name)}, where it went from {fmt(top.began)} to {fmt(top.total)} years: a
    rise of {fmt(top.change)} years.
    {y && <> Over the last year alone it rose in {y.up.length} of {y.rows.length} countries and fell in {y.down.length}.</>}
  </p>

  <h2>From {fmt(h.earliest.total)} to {fmt(h.latest.total)} years</h2>
  <p>
    The longest expected working life is in {W(h.latest.name)}, at {fmt(h.latest.total)} years. The shortest
    is in {W(h.earliest.name)}, at {fmt(h.earliest.total)}. That is a difference of {fmt(h.gap)} years in
    the same continent.
  </p>

  <h2>{baltic ? "Women's working lives are shorter, except in the Baltic states" : "Women's working lives are shorter in most countries"}</h2>
  <p>
    In {g.womenLower} of the {g.of} countries women can expect a shorter working life than men, by
    {' '}{fmt(g.mean)} years on average. The widest gap is in {W(g.widest.name)}: {fmt(g.widestGap)} years.
    {womenLonger.length > 0 && <> Women can expect to work longer than men in {womenLonger.join(', ').replace(/, ([^,]*)$/, ' and $1')}.</>}
  </p>

  <h2>What this figure is, and what it is not</h2>
  <p>
    This is an expectation, not a count of finished careers. Eurostat calculates how many years a
    15-year-old would spend in the labour market if today's pattern of work at every age stayed the
    same, the way life expectancy is calculated. It counts years in work or looking for work, not
    hours, and it says nothing about the age at which people retire.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nor do the figures say why working lives grew. That would need data on pensions, education and
    employment that is not in this dataset, so this note does not guess.
  </p>
</ArticleShell>
